#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bell_b

#% text
# `bell_b(n)` gives the Bell number, the number of partitions of an `n`-element set; `bell_b(n, x)` gives the Bell polynomial.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` an integer `>= 0`; the numbers come from Bell's triangle with exact integers, so any size up to index 40000 is exact. With a symbolic `x` the result is the polynomial `sum(stirling_s2(n, k) x^k)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bell_b(5)
#% output
#= 52

#% cell
bell_b(range(0, 8))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 5, 15, 52, 203, 877, 4140]

#% cell
bell_b(100)
#% output
#= 47585391276764833658790768841387207826363669686825611466616334637559114497892442622672724044217756306953557882560751

#% cell
bell_b(4, x)
#% output
#= x + 7*x^2 + 6*x^3 + x^4

#% cell
bell_b(3, 2)
#% output
#= 22

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `stirling_s2`, `partitions_p`, `bernoulli_b`
